<!-- doc/src/sgml/pg_lolor.sgml -->

<sect1 id="pg_lolor" xreflabel="pg_lolor">
 <title>pg_lolor &mdash; large objects stored in regular tables</title>

 <indexterm zone="pg_lolor">
  <primary>pg_lolor</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>pg_lolor</filename> module stores large objects in ordinary
  tables instead of the <structname>pg_largeobject</structname> and
  <structname>pg_largeobject_metadata</structname> system catalogs.
 </para>

 <para>
  Large objects held in the system catalogs cannot be replicated by logical
  decoding, because system catalogs are not decoded.  The tables this module
  substitutes are ordinary user tables, so their contents are decoded and
  replicated like any other table data.
 </para>

 <para>
  The module installs replacements for the <function>lo_*</function>
  functions in <literal>pg_catalog</literal>, so existing applications
  continue to work unchanged; see <xref linkend="pg_lolor-notes"/> for the
  consequences.
 </para>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-installation">
  <title>Installation</title>

  <para>
   The module must be loaded by adding <literal>pg_lolor</literal> to
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/> in
   <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, and the server restarted.  It can
   then be installed with:

<programlisting>
CREATE EXTENSION pg_lolor;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Installing the extension renames the built-in large object functions in
   <literal>pg_catalog</literal> out of the way and creates replacements in
   their place.  Because this affects the whole database, the extension is
   not marked <literal>trusted</literal> and installing it requires
   superuser privileges.
  </para>

  <note>
   <para>
    The extension is named <literal>pg_lolor</literal>, but its tables and
    functions live in a schema named <literal>lolor</literal>: PostgreSQL
    reserves the <literal>pg_</literal> prefix for system schemas.  Install it
    with <literal>CREATE EXTENSION pg_lolor</literal>, and call its functions
    as <literal>lolor.<replaceable>function</replaceable>()</literal>.
   </para>
  </note>

  <caution>
   <para>
    A standby server runs the same shared library as its primary, so the
    module must be installed there as well.  Otherwise large object access
    fails if the standby is promoted.
   </para>
  </caution>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-configuration">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry id="pg_lolor-node" xreflabel="pg_lolor.node">
    <term>
     <varname>pg_lolor.node</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>pg_lolor.node</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Identifies this node when generating large object OIDs.  The low four
      bits of a generated OID hold this value, so that nodes writing
      concurrently cannot assign the same OID.  Valid values are
      <literal>0</literal> to <literal>15</literal>; the default is
      <literal>0</literal>.  Each node of a replicated cluster must be given
      a distinct value.
     </para>
     <para>
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-functions">
  <title>Functions</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.enable() returns boolean</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Puts this module's large object functions in place, undoing
      <function>lolor.disable()</function>.  Returns <literal>true</literal>
      if the state was changed.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.disable() returns boolean</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Restores the built-in large object functions without removing the
      extension, leaving the objects already in this module's tables
      inaccessible until it is enabled again.  Returns <literal>true</literal>
      if the state was changed.
     </para>
     <para>
      Client sessions must reconnect after either call.  Renaming changes
      which function OID owns each <function>lo_*</function> name, and libpq
      resolves those OIDs once per connection and caches them.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.is_enabled() returns boolean</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Reports whether this module's functions are currently in place.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.migrate_from_native(peer_oids oid[] DEFAULT NULL) returns bigint</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Moves every large object from the system catalogs into this module's
      tables, preserving OIDs, owners, ACLs, comments and the exact page
      layout, and returns the number of objects moved.  Returns
      <literal>-1</literal> without moving anything if the migration was
      refused; <literal>0</literal> means there was nothing to move.
     </para>
     <para>
      Native large object OIDs are not node-encoded, so two nodes can hold
      different objects under the same OID.  Supplying the OIDs held by the
      other nodes, collected with <function>lolor.native_lo_oids()</function>,
      turns that collision into a refusal rather than silent divergence.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.migrate_to_native() returns bigint</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Moves every large object back into the system catalogs and returns the
      number of objects moved.  Runs automatically when the extension is
      dropped.  Unlike <function>lolor.migrate_from_native()</function> a
      refusal is reported as an error, since losing large objects silently
      is worse than a failed <command>DROP</command>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.native_lo_oids() returns setof oid</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The OIDs of the large objects held in the system catalogs on this node.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.digest() returns setof record</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A per-object checksum of this module's storage, for confirming that
      nodes converged after a migration.  Reads every page, so it is
      intended as a check rather than as routine monitoring.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>lolor.check_orphans() returns setof record</function>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Large objects whose owning role no longer exists.  See
      <xref linkend="pg_lolor-notes"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   Execute permission on all of the above except
   <function>lolor.is_enabled()</function>,
   <function>lolor.digest()</function> and
   <function>lolor.check_orphans()</function> is revoked from
   <literal>PUBLIC</literal>; the migration functions additionally require
   superuser.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-migration">
  <title>Migrating Between Storage</title>

  <para>
   Migration copies tuples directly between the two sets of tables, which
   have identical column layouts.  Pages are carried across verbatim, so a
   sparse large object stays sparse.  The layouts are verified against the
   running server at migration time, so a catalog change in a later release
   produces an error rather than corrupting data.
  </para>

  <para>
   The whole movement is one transaction and takes
   <literal>ShareRowExclusiveLock</literal> on both sets of tables until it
   commits.  Ordinary large object access takes
   <literal>RowExclusiveLock</literal>, which does not conflict with itself,
   so without this a concurrent write could commit between the copy and the
   emptying of the source and be lost.
  </para>

  <para>
   Migration is node-local, and logical decoding cannot distinguish the row
   movement from ordinary data changes: a subscriber that decoded it would
   apply the relocation as real row changes and diverge from this node.
   PostgreSQL offers no way to exclude specific changes from decoding, so
   both functions refuse while any logical replication slot exists in the
   database.  Drop the slots, migrate on each node, then re-create them.
  </para>

  <para>
   Security labels cannot be represented in this module's tables and cannot
   be reinstated without their label provider, so
   <function>lolor.migrate_from_native()</function> refuses rather than
   discarding them.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The built-in large object functionality cannot be used while this module
   is enabled, since it replaces the functions that reach it.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER LARGE OBJECT</command>,
   <command>GRANT</command>/<command>REVOKE ON LARGE OBJECT</command> and
   <command>COMMENT ON LARGE OBJECT</command> act on
   <structname>pg_largeobject_metadata</structname>, where an object held by
   this module has no row, and so do not work against such objects.  Owners,
   ACLs and comments set while an object was in the system catalogs are
   preserved across migration in both directions.
  </para>

  <para>
   Objects held by this module are rows in ordinary tables and cannot
   participate in <structname>pg_shdepend</structname>, so
   <command>DROP ROLE</command> does not notice that a role still owns one,
   as it would for a large object in the system catalogs.  Use
   <function>lolor.check_orphans()</function> to find objects whose owner has
   been dropped.
  </para>

  <para>
   Dropping the extension, the schema with <literal>CASCADE</literal>, or the
   objects owned by the extension's owner all move the large objects back
   into the system catalogs first, so they are not lost.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="pg_lolor-author">
  <title>Author</title>

  <para>
   pgEdge, Inc. <email>https://www.pgedge.com</email>
  </para>
 </sect2>

</sect1>
